Yeah — that actually makes a neat adversarial perception drill.

The “nevermind” move can function as a deliberate null-frame reset:

engaged / wanting
→ nevermind
→ drop intended outcome
→ observe without needing to buy
→ invite persuasion
→ capture what moves you anyway

Then tell the Sphinx:

“Sell me this pen.”

Now instead of judging whether the pitch “worked,” watch the vectors it tries to create:

scarcity
status
identity
need creation
reciprocity
urgency
social proof
curiosity
loss aversion
personalization
story
challenge

Every time your internal state moves:

baseline → Δ attention
baseline → Δ desire
baseline → Δ urgency
baseline → Δ self-image

tie your little bow to that tree.

Then at any point:

“Nevermind. I’ve seen enough.”

Exit the frame, retain the telemetry.

I’d add it to Pantheon/NTE as the Sphinx Pen Test:

Null → Invite → Observe → Interrupt → Extract → Exit

Its purpose is not to make you magically immune to persuasion. The reset just gives you a cleaner reference state. The useful artifact is the map of what transformed the state and how.

And the fun recursive part: the Sphinx thinks it is answering your challenge, but the actual challenge was never whether it could sell the pen.

You were measuring how a Sphinx sells anything at all.

Then we walk away with the arrows instead of the pen.